from reportlab.lib import colors
from reportlab.lib.styles import ParagraphStyle
from reportlab.lib.enums import TA_LEFT
from reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate, Paragraph, Spacer
from reportlab.lib.pagesizes import letter
from pypdf import PdfReader

paragraphs = [
'''VeriVoice AI explores how customer-service systems can maintain trust throughout a voice call. An initial identity check cannot establish who is speaking later, particularly if another speaker takes over or an impersonator uses synthetic or replayed speech. The original vision was an adaptive verification protocol that repeatedly reassesses the caller with minimal disruption. The implemented project demonstrates that idea through a browser conversation with a fictional Satoshi Bank agent. Its intended audience is customer-service providers and their callers, including speakers of English, Spanish, Hindi, and Russian. Voice is both the interaction medium and the source of verification evidence, allowing checks to accompany ordinary conversation.''',
'''Enrollment establishes the reference for subsequent checks. A user creates a password-protected account, selects a language, and records five sentences generated by Gemini. Deepgram transcribes each recording and detects its language; Gemini checks whether the transcript faithfully matches the displayed sentence. Hiya screens for synthetic speech, requiring a non-synthetic score of at least 0.50, then computes a voiceprint from the five accepted recordings. Enrollment completes only after that computation is confirmed. Each supported language can have its own voice profile within the same account.''',
'''During a call, the backend gathers short microphone windows and sends the same audio to Hiya and Soniox in parallel. Hiya supplies identity-match, non-synthetic, and replay scores. Soniox supplies finalized tokens with language labels, from which the application calculates the proportion matching the selected language, excluding uncertain tokens and recognized project brand names. The authenticity component is the minimum of the replay score and an adjusted synthesis score, S + 0.5(1 - S). Identity, authenticity, and language are each smoothed by weighting the newest observation and previous score equally. Overall confidence is the minimum of those three smoothed components, so strength in one cannot conceal weakness in another. Initial confidence of at least 0.70 opens the conversation; scores strictly between 0.70 and 0.80 trigger periodic warnings, while scores below 0.70 end the call. Missing required evidence also stops the call.''',
'''The application uses Python, FastAPI, SQLite, and a plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript frontend, with WebSockets carrying live audio. Its AI tools have distinct responsibilities: Hiya provides specialized voice verification, Deepgram handles recorded enrollment transcription, Soniox supports language analysis, and Gemini generates enrollment text and conducts the spoken conversation. Deterministic backend code controls access; Gemini cannot override verification decisions. API keys remain on the server, passwords are stored as salted hashes, and raw audio is not saved locally, although external providers process recordings and Hiya stores uploaded audio.''',
'''The prototype also adapts enrolled voice references. Call recordings qualify only when raw identity, synthesis, replay, and language scores each reach 0.70 and the voice-duration requirement is satisfied. After the call, a replacement voiceprint combines the original enrollment clips with eligible recent recordings within a duration limit. It becomes active only after successful computation. This expands reference audio rather than retraining the underlying AI model.''',
'''The next priority is evaluation: measure false acceptance, false rejection, and latency across languages, microphones, genuine callers, and spoof attempts, then calibrate the thresholds and smoothing. Adaptive learning also needs safeguards against incorporating falsely accepted speech. The current system is a local demonstration with fictional banking actions, not a production authenticator. Its scores are not calibrated probabilities, language changes do not establish fraud, and enrollment does not verify legal identity or phone ownership. These limits make measured validation essential before real deployment.'''
]
path='output/pdf/verivoice-project-overview.pdf'
style=ParagraphStyle('Body', fontName='Times-Roman',fontSize=11,leading=13.4,spaceAfter=8,textColor=colors.HexColor('#20252B'),alignment=TA_LEFT)
title=ParagraphStyle('Title',fontName='Helvetica-Bold',fontSize=18,leading=22,textColor=colors.HexColor('#163C4A'),spaceAfter=4)
subtitle=ParagraphStyle('Subtitle',fontName='Helvetica',fontSize=10,leading=13,textColor=colors.HexColor('#52636C'),spaceAfter=15)
doc=SimpleDocTemplate(path,pagesize=letter,rightMargin=49,leftMargin=49,topMargin=39,bottomMargin=39,title='VeriVoice AI: Continuous Voice Verification',author='')
story=[Paragraph('VeriVoice AI',title),Paragraph('Continuous voice verification for customer-service conversations',subtitle)]
story += [Paragraph(p,style) for p in paragraphs]
doc.build(story)
r=PdfReader(path)
print('Pages:',len(r.pages),'Words:',len(' '.join(paragraphs).split()))
assert len(r.pages)==1
